TI  - Intermittent Fasting as a Nonpharmacologic Strategy to Manage Polycystic Ovary 
      Syndrome.
PG  - 139-147
LID - S1751-4851(25)00219-3 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.nwh.2025.10.003 [doi]
AB  - Polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS) is a metabolic disorder that affects 6% to 21% 
      of women of reproductive age. It is characterized by hyperandrogenism, 
      ano/oligo-ovulation, and ovarian cysts. Insulin resistance is a common finding 
      among women with PCOS. To effectively manage PCOS, efforts must be focused on 
      improving insulin resistance, as it has been noted to play a major role in a 
      multitude of life-altering and life-threatening conditions. Intermittent fasting 
      (IF) is a dietary intervention alternating between fasting and feasting. IF has 
      been shown to improve insulin resistance, hyperinsulinemia, and hyperandrogenism, 
      all of which contribute to the hormone imbalance and reproductive complications 
      seen in women with PCOS. Current literature will be reviewed to demonstrate that 
      IF may be a safe, nonpharmacologic intervention to manage PCOS.
