TI  - Insulin resistance and oxidative marker in women with PCOS.
PG  - 183-186
LID - 10.1080/13813455.2018.1499120 [doi]
AB  - Polycystic Ovary Syndrome is a multifactorial reproductive problem and a leading 
      cause of female infertility worldwide. Evidences have shown that Oxidative Stress 
      and decreased antioxidant status are often linked with PCOS. Insulin Resistance 
      in PCOS patients ranges from 50% to 70% and may encourage OS by production of 
      reactive oxygen species.Objective: Our study determines serum MDA levels along 
      with plasma glucose, serum insulin, and insulin resistance in obese and nonobese 
      PCOS subjects.Materials and methods: A case control study was conducted on 
      diagnosed 100 PCOS patients and 100 controls. Fasting plasma glucose was measured 
      by enzymatic method. Insulin was estimated by chemiluminescent microparticle 
      immunoassay using Abott Architect i 2000 SR analyser. Insulin resistance was 
      calculated by HOMA-IR. Malonaldehyde is determined as Thiobarbituric acid 
      reactive substances.Results: CRP and serum MDA levels were increased in women 
      with PCOS irrespective of obesity compared to their respective controls with a p 
      value of < .001. However, though fasting glucose, serum insulin, and IR were 
      increased in both obese and nonobese women with PCOS compared to their BMI 
      adjusted controls with p value of < .001, the values were within reference range 
      in nonobese women.Conclusion: Our study suggests that women with PCOS have 
      oxidative stress and elevated CRP irrespective of obesity. However, 
      hyperinsulinemia and Insulin resistance are seen only in obese women with PCOS, 
      indicating that these women are at high risk for developing low grade 
      inflammation and cardiovascular diseases.
