TI  - Macrophage migration inhibitory factor in obese and non obese women with 
      polycystic ovary syndrome.
PG  - 31-7
LID - S1575-0922(14)00251-4 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.endonu.2014.09.005 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To measure macrophage migration inhibitory factor (MIF) concentrations 
      in obese and non-obese women diagnosed with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). 
      METHODS: Women diagnosed with PCOS and age-matched healthy controls with regular 
      menses and normal ovaries on ultrasound examination were selected and divided 
      into 4 groups (group A, PCOS and obese; group B, PCOS and non-obese; group C, 
      obese controls; and group D, non-obese controls) based on body mass index (obese 
      >30 kg/m2 and non-obese <25 kg/m2). Luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating 
      hormone, androstenedione, testosterone, sex hormone-binding globulin, serum 
      glucose, insulin and MIF levels were measured. RESULTS: Obese and non-obese women 
      with PCOS had higher luteinizing hormone, follicle-stimulating hormone, 
      androstenedione, testosterone, and insulin levels as compared to the obese and 
      non-obese control groups, respectively (P < .0001). Women with PCOS had 
      significantly higher MIF levels (group A, 48.6 ± 9.9 mg/ml; group B, 35.2 ± 6.0 
      ng/ml) as compared to controls (group C, 13.5 ± 6.0 ng/ml; group D, 12.0 ± 4.3 
      ng/dl; P < .0001). A weak, positive and significant correlation was seen between 
      fasting blood glucose and insulin levels in women with PCOS (P < .05). 
      CONCLUSION: Significant differences exist in plasma MIF levels between obese and 
      non-obese women with and without PCOS.
