DP  - 2026 Jun 7
TI  - Deconstructing triglyceride glucose-body mass index in normal-weight women with 
      polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome.
LID - S0015-0282(26)00492-9 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.fertnstert.2026.06.013 [doi]
AB  - OBJECTIVE: To study the biological pathways underlying the triglyceride 
      glucose-body mass index (TyG-BMI), calculated as natural logarithm (plasma 
      triglyceride [mg/dL] × fasting blood glucose [mg/dL]/2) × body mass index (BMI), 
      in normal-weight women with polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome (PMOS) and 
      age-/BMI-matched normoandrogenic ovulatory (control) women, and to determine 
      whether the TyG-BMI in such women is a useful clinical predictor of reduced in 
      vitro fertilization (IVF) pregnancy outcome. DESIGN: Retrospective cohort study 
      SUBJECTS: Twenty-one normal-weight women with PMOS and 19 age- and BMI-matched 
      controls. EXPOSURE: All women underwent fasting circulating metabolic/androgenic 
      determinations. MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES: The TyG-BMI was compared between women 
      with PMOS and age-/BMI-matched controls and correlated with metabolic/androgenic 
      outcomes. RESULTS: Serum luteinizing hormone/androgen levels and fasting serum 
      triglyceride/insulin levels were significantly greater in women with PMOS than 
      controls. Adipose insulin resistance (adipose-IR) was also increased in women 
      with PMOS vs. controls. The TyG-BMI and homeostasis model assessment of insulin 
      resistance, however, were not significantly different by female type. In 
      multivariable linear regression, TyG-BMI was unassociated with PMOS, serum free 
      testosterone or fasting serum insulin levels. Instead, in all women combined, 
      higher TyG-BMI levels were associated with greater adipose-IR, higher serum 
      non-high-density lipoprotein cholesterol, and lower serum high-density 
      lipoprotein cholesterol levels. CONCLUSION: Adipose-IR accompanying altered lipid 
      metabolism underlies the TyG-BMI of normal-weight women with PMOS and 
      age-/BMI-matched controls. The TyG-BMI, however, may have limited utility in 
      predicting reduced IVF pregnancy outcome in normal-weight women with PMOS, 
      although confirmation in similar cohorts undergoing IVF is needed.
